== E-graph refresher: computing congruence closure

- Keep track of equivalence classes (E-classes) with a union-find

#pause

- Rather than representing terms, represent E-nodes (e.g. $f(e_1, e_2)$)

== E-graph refresher: computing congruence closure

To insert a term: flatten it leaf-to-root, looking up or inserting an equivalence class
for each E-node

== E-graph refresher: computing congruence closure

To merge terms: call union() on the union-find

== E-graph refresher: computing congruence closure

To compute the congruence closure, we have to do rebuild()

#pause

1. For every E-node $f(e_1, e_2, ...)$, update it to $f("find"(e_1), "find"(e_2))$

#pause

2. Whenever we have $f(e_1, e_2, ...) in e_j$ and also $in e_k$, call union($e_j, e_k$)

#pause

A bit slow (maybe). But we can defer rebuilding and/or use Downey-Sethi-Tarjan (aka binarizing the function symbols)

== E-graph refresher: equality saturation

Congruence closure decides the theory of ground equations. What about identities?

== E-graph refresher: equality saturation

Idea: if we have $forall x ..., t = s$, try instantiating the quantifier with known E-classes.

_Surely_ if we have some equational proof we want to find, it will involve identities instantiated at ground terms in this obvious way.

#pause

The truth is a little more subtle, but I will gloss over this.

== Equality saturation

After instantiation, we have a ground equation $t' = s'$.

We can just insert this into the graph and run congruence closure/rebuilding.

== Equality saturation

Is this complete? Not always. Another complicated story...

#pause

But a key takeaway here is that if $t'$ and $s'$ are already represented, equating them _shrinks_ the E-graph.
But inserting new things can grow the E-graph arbitrarily.

== E-graphs vs AC

The bane of equality saturation is operators that are AC or similar (ACU, abelian groups, etc.).

For simplicity, we will use '+' as a generic name for an AC operator.

