== E-graphs as rewrite systems
E-graphs connect very closely to the theory of *rewriting*.

The 'Abstract Congruence Closure' perspective is that E-graphs are building a
_convergent term rewriting system_ on an extended signature.

Congruence closure is very similar to 'ground Knuth-Bendix completion' from rewriting.

(Equality saturation is a bit of a stranger animal but still vaguely completion-flavoured)

== E-graphs as tree automata
E-graphs are reachable deterministic bottom-up tree automata

- Reachable: every E-class is inhabited by some E-node
- Deterministic: if $f(s_1, s_2) arrow.r s_3$ and $f(s_1, s_2) arrow.r s_4$ then $s_3 = s_4$
- Bottom-up: there is a notion of top-down deterministic tree automata but it is weaker than deterministic bottom-up (compare the string automata case, where left-to-right and right-to-left are equivalent)

== Inspirations for adding the "mod T"
Rewriting modulo theories

Algebraic automata (that is, tree automata on non-free theories)

== The neat thing about E-graphs
...is that they add a new symbol for everything.
$f(e_1)$? Well, if we haven't seen that before, give it a new E-class/tree-automata state

== Confluence
If we have a term that is 'in multiple E-classes', we ought to merge the E-classes.

This is what rebuilding does:

$e_1 arrow.r e_2$ and $f(... e_1 ...) arrow.r e_3$ ('parent-child overlap')

$f(... e_1 ...) arrow.r e_3$ and $f(... e_1 ...) arrow.r e_4$ ('self-overlap')

The new symbols for E-classes means we can handle granchild overlap, etc, by iterating these basic steps.

== ...which is how they simplify confluence
If we have a critical pair of terms, they must have some relevant 'overlap' between rules.
Any overlap must be a case of subterm containment. Every subterm gets a new symbol, so we only have to check for a few cases:

$e_1 arrow.r e_2$ and $f(... e_1 ...) arrow.r e_3$ ('parent-child overlap')

$f(... e_1 ...) arrow.r e_3$ and $f(... e_1 ...) arrow.r e_4$ ('self-overlap')

Grandchild overlap, etc, is handled by fixpoint propagation.

== Key notion: critical pairs
A critical pair is some minimal term that can rewrite in two ways/is in two E-classes. To do congruence closure
(modulo $T$) properly, we need to find critical pairs and resolve them, by merging the E-classes.

// == ...which is less neat for AC
== AC-critical pairs
$
(a + b) + c = e_1 \
b + (c + d) = e_2
$

$b + c$ is the _overlap_ of an AC-critical pair:

$
e_1 + d =_(A C) a + b + c + d =_(A C) a + e_2
$

What are we doing in AC equality saturation? Well, any of the $O(2^n)$ AC-subterms _could_ become an AC-critical pair.
We are eagerly giving new E-classes to them. Just in case. After all,
equality saturation only works by grounding identities: we have not given a mechanism to ground something only when it's actually needed.

== EMTs and the word problem
Any mechanism to automatically resolve all $T$-critical pairs is a solver for the $T$-word problem.

(Resolving critical pairs = scanning for implied equalities)

== EMTs, prehistorically
Existing suggestions for EM(T)s fall along two very similar lines:

- Canonize the $T$ parts of the E-graph, and work mod $T$ in some way
- Treat E-nodes as 'containers' (of E-class names), and move from fixed-arity function symbols to lists, multisets/bags, etc

Either way is an attempt to bake the theories in:

- A(U) = semigroups = lists = associate to the right
- AC(U) = commutative semigroups = multisets/bags = sort ascending and associate to the right

== EMTs, the end of prehistory

The intuition:

'Why do we need all these AC-equivalent nodes? Can't we just pick an AC-representative?'

Yes, _but_

You need some method of finding $T$-critical pairs, and to make a complete algorithm, you must _eventually_ resolve every pair.
And also match modulo $T$ to instantiate identities.

== Counterexamples to prehistoric EMTs
$
a + b = a
$

Modulo A, we ought to generate the *infinite* set of implied subterms $b, b + b, ...$. So, prehistoric methods fail when E-graphs are *not flat terms*.

== Counterexamples to prehistoric EMTs
$
b + a + c = a
$

The infinite set of equivalent terms modulo A is now $n b + a + n c$. We can no longer recognize the
set of all re-associations of this, or the set of canonized forms of this, with a tree automata.

== Counterexamples to prehistoric EMTs
$
a + (b + c) = d \
a + b = e \
e + c = f
$

Prehistoric methods might canonize, but this means they don't scan for implied equalities among a set of (canonized!) equations.
Actually...

== A and AC
Finding A-critical pairs, and matching modulo A, is quite feasible. Still, there is not 'packaged' solution possible:
the A-word problem is undecidable. So, the best we can do is just provide a list of critical pairs for the 'outer loop' of equality saturation
to choose to resolve (or not)

AC is better: the word problem is decidable (Mayr-Meyer). It's ESPACE-complete...
Sounds bad, but E-graphs are EESPACE space here, but more importantly: we can solve common cases of
the AC word problem faster than ESPACE (hopefully), whereas E-graphs _must_ chug along to saturation to be _certain_
(as they have no smart way of knowing if some AC-subterm is relevant to the critical pair problem)

== The AC word problem, a la Bachmair et al.

Given $a_1 + a_2 + ... arrow.r e_1$ and $b_1 + b_2 + ... arrow.r e_2$, construct

$
e_1 + ... = a_1 + a_2 + ... + b_1 + b_2 = ... + e_2
$

Orient it by some (multiset) ordering and add it as a rule.

== The AC word problem, the polynomial encoding
If $a b c = d e f$, encode this as a polynomial equation $x_a x_b x_c - x_d x_e x_f = 0$.

This is not overkill, since we have the ESPACE lower bound...

#pause

A set of AC equations form a _polynomial ideal_, and finding a reduced _basis_ for this ideal solves the word problem. And this is also what algebraists
have been doing for a long time: Buchberger's algorithm and Gröbner bases are standard tools

== AC word problem example, as an ideal
$
(a + b) + c = e_1 \
b + (c + d) = e_2 \
"translate" => \
a b c - e_1 = 0 \
b c d - e_2 = 0 \
=> \
a b c d - d(a b c - e_1) = -e_1 d \
a b c d - a(b c d - e_2) = -e_2 a \
$

== AC word problem example, as an ideal
$
e_1 d - e_2 a = 0
$

Final basis:

$
a b c - e_1 = 0 \
b c d - e_2 = 0 \
e_1 d - e_2 a = 0
$

== Buchberger etc

Buchberger's algorithm, and the refined $F_4$ and $F_5$ variants, are what we would like to use.

How to integrate their term-ordering fiddlyness with our flattened E-class system?

Well, Tiwari solved it in his dissertation sections on congruence closure mod AC.

It turns out E-graphs have yet to fully absorb all the insights of the term-rewriting world.

We still need AC-matching, though.

== Buchberger again

We can solve not just AC, but rings, polynomials, ACU, etc, with the same techniques. So, even if there is a purely "term"-based approach
(Buchberger and Tiwari, _Congruence Closure modulo AC_)

== Better than Buchberger
Abelian groups (aka ACUN) can be solved with Hermite normal form. Polynomial time!
Vector spaces over a field can be solved with Gaussian elimination, which is $O(n^(2."something"))$

== Matching modulo AC: semantics
There is the delicate matter that *what* is considered an $A C$-match could affect completeness.

For instance, if we have the pattern $x + f(x)$, should we consider just the substitutions
$x := t$ where $t + f(t)$ is in the E-graph, or do we have to broaden to consider this when
$t$ and $f(t)$, but not $t + f(t)$, are in the E-graph?

== Matching modulo AC and Gröbner bases
Our actual representation of AC-terms to match against is not even in the form of a bunch of E-classes and E-nodes, but some
incrementally-updated Gröbner basis system, which affects what "in the E-graph" means.

The important limitation: we will only find matches where the original E-graph with AC identities would.

So, there is a fairly reasonable definition of matches even for this.

== Loose ends 1: group UF
There is a notion of group union find: instead of representing a bunch of equations
$a = b = c$, we can represent $a = g b = h^(-1) c$. The group structure corresponds to reflexivity, symmetry, and transitivity.

This is also some form of "EMT" although a very different thing.

Some extensions to the group UF have been recently proposed, and a unifying version is still needed.

== Loose ends 2: functorial UF
My proposal, that a 'weighted UF' is a functor from a thin groupoid to (a subgroupoid of) some other category, hasn't been widely accepted :P

I ought to write the details down sometime to make it more convincing.

== Loose ends 3: frontiers in E-graphs
- Abstract interpretation (AI), Galois connections, and E-graphs (AI is the perspective taken in the most recent reference for weighted UFs)
- SSA and E-graphs: coinduction vs induction, sea-of-nodes, representing control flow
- E-graphs all the way down in compilers. We can do register allocation directly on SSA, why not directly on E-graphs? The dream
  is some integer-linear-program approximate solver for extracting programs quickly from E-graphs, and an exact solver for the -O4 usecase.
- Coloured E-graphs and conditional rewriting. Coloured E-graphs ought to be a monotonic function from a poset to the equivalence relation.
- Slotted E-graphs and binding
- Non-ground rewriting. Find better identities directly rather than slogging through proving their consequences on ground terms. Maybe if we keep a provenance of
  why ground terms are equal, we can automatically find interesting non-ground identities.
- Formalizing extraction as weighted automata
- Formalizing E-class analyses (ought to be not too hard)
- E-graphs for monoidal categories
- E-graphs for regular languages (they are non-algebraically-axiomatizable, in some sense)

== Loose ends 4: decision procedures
- 'Fulls seldom differ' (decision procedure)
- Presburger arithmetic (it's just cool)
- Mixed boolean arithmetic and automata for deciding the linear fragment (!!)
- Bitvectors and arrays and other stuff from SMT-land
- Pointer reasoning?
- Inequality and disequality reasoning?
- Quantifier elimination, the decision procedure for the reals
- Integer linear programming, Graver or Hilbert bases
- Gosper's algorithm, aka A=B
- Risch's algorithm 

== Loose ends 5: scribbles
Various things I would like to think more about

Automata, automatic groups, other solvable $T$s for EM(T), other decision procedures, quantifier instantiation, faster
indexing for WCOJs...
